<article
class="entry-3398 post type-post st-publish format-standard has-post-thumbnail entry category-salon-marketing h-article-single" id="entry-3398">

<div class="h-post-format- single-layout-1">

		<header class="entry-header">

			<h1 class="entry-title">How to Get More Google Reviews for Your Dubai Salon (Without Breaking Google&#8217;s Rules)</h1><div class="entry-meta">By <span class="posted-by vcard author">			<a title="View all posts by Abu Bakar"
				href="/our-blog/" rel="author"
				class="url fn n"				>
				<span
				class="author-name"				>
				Abu Bakar			</span>
			</a>
		</span>

		 / <span class="posted-on"><span class="published"> October 6, 2026 </span></span></div>

		</header>

	<div class="entry-content clear"
	>

		<figure class="blog-hero"><img fetchpriority="high" width="11520" height="9216" decoding="async" src="/images/2026/10/salon-google-reviews-dubai-canva-cover.png" alt="A five-star rating cluster with a map pin and a QR code — getting more Google reviews for a Dubai salon" style="width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:16px;" /></figure>
<p>Picture two salons a few streets apart in Dubai. Both do good work. One has 40 Google reviews, the other has 400. When a client searches &#8220;hair salon near me&#8221; and has to choose between them in about four seconds, the reviews usually decide it, long before anyone looks at the Instagram feed or the price list.</p>
<p>Most salon owners know this and still don&#8217;t ask. They feel awkward, they forget, or they once tried a sign at the front desk and nothing happened. The other temptation is worse: a shortcut like a discount for a five-star review, or a service that promises &#8220;50 reviews this week&#8221;. Those shortcuts break Google&#8217;s rules and can get reviews removed or a profile restricted.</p>
<p>This guide covers the honest version: how to ask, when to ask, what to say, and what to avoid, so your review count grows steadily and stays safe.</p>
<div class="gd-takeaway">
<h4>🔑 Key takeaways</h4>
<ul>
<li>You are allowed to ask for reviews. Google&#8217;s own guidance suggests sharing a review link or QR code with customers.</li>
<li>You are not allowed to reward reviews. Free or discounted services in exchange for a review are classed as fake engagement.</li>
<li>Ask every client the same way. Sending only happy clients to Google (review gating) goes against the principle that reviews must reflect genuine experiences.</li>
<li>A simple habit beats a big campaign: one personal ask from the stylist, a link in WhatsApp, and a reply to every review.</li>
</ul>
</div>
<div class="gd-toc">
<h4>📑 In this guide</h4>
<ol>
<li><a href="#why-reviews-matter">Why reviews decide the booking</a></li>
<li><a href="#rules-you-must-follow">The rules you must follow</a></li>
<li><a href="#when-and-how-to-ask">When and how to ask</a></li>
<li><a href="#what-to-say">What to say (WhatsApp scripts)</a></li>
<li><a href="#replying-to-reviews">Replying to every review</a></li>
<li><a href="#mistakes-to-avoid">Mistakes that get reviews removed</a></li>
<li><a href="#salon-google-reviews-faq">FAQ</a></li>
</ol>
</div>
<h2 id="why-reviews-matter">Why Reviews Decide the Booking</h2>
<p>Reviews do two jobs at once. First, they are part of how Google decides which salons to show in the map results, alongside relevance and distance, which we explain in <a href="/salon-not-showing-google-maps/">why your salon doesn&#8217;t show up in &#8220;near me&#8221; searches</a>. Second, they are the proof a new client reads before she trusts you with her hair, nails or skin.</p>
<p>What matters is not only the star average. Clients read the most recent reviews and look for specifics: the stylist&#8217;s name, the service, whether the result matched the photo she brought. A review like &#8220;Aisha did my balayage and it looked like the reference picture&#8221; does more work than ten that say &#8220;great service&#8221;.</p>
<h2 id="rules-you-must-follow">The Rules You Must Follow</h2>
<p>Google publishes its position clearly, and it&#8217;s worth reading before you build any process around reviews. In its Business Profile help, <a href="https://support.google.com/business/answer/3474122" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google says you can ask customers to visit a Google link or scan a QR code</a> to leave a review. The same page says that offering incentives, such as free or discounted goods or services, in exchange for posting, changing or removing reviews is considered fake and misleading content and is strictly prohibited.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Do:</strong> ask every client, share your official review link or QR code, and make it easy.</li>
<li><strong>Don&#8217;t:</strong> offer a discount, free add-on, raffle entry or loyalty points in return for a review.</li>
<li><strong>Don&#8217;t:</strong> ask staff, friends or family to post reviews for clients they never served.</li>
<li><strong>Don&#8217;t:</strong> buy reviews from a service. If a vendor promises a fixed number of reviews, treat that as a red flag.</li>
<li><strong>Don&#8217;t:</strong> filter who gets asked. If you send only the clients you expect to be happy, you are gating reviews, and it distorts the genuine feedback Google expects.</li>
</ul>
<p>You can generate your link or QR code from your profile: Google&#8217;s steps are in its guide to <a href="https://support.google.com/business/answer/16816815" target="_blank" rel="noopener">creating a Google link or QR code to request reviews</a>. Print the QR code on a small card at the reception desk and at each station mirror, and keep the link saved on the salon&#8217;s WhatsApp.</p>
<div class="gd-hook"><strong>⚡ 30-second check:</strong> Open your Google Business Profile and look at your last 10 reviews. How many are older than three months, and how many mention a stylist or a service by name? If the answer is &#8220;most are old&#8221; and &#8220;almost none&#8221;, the fix is a steady habit, not a campaign. <a href="/get-a-free-audit/">Want us to look at your profile? Get a free audit →</a></div>
<h2 id="when-and-how-to-ask">When and How to Ask</h2>
<p>Timing and person matter more than wording. A request feels natural when it comes from the person who just did the work, at the moment the client is looking in the mirror and smiling.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>At the chair:</strong> the stylist says something like &#8220;If you were happy with it, a short Google review really helps us. I&#8217;ll send you the link on WhatsApp.&#8221;</li>
<li><strong>On WhatsApp, same day:</strong> a short message with the link, sent within a few hours while the visit is fresh. Connect this to the follow-up routine in our <a href="/salon-client-retention-loyalty-dubai/">client retention guide</a> so it isn&#8217;t a separate task someone has to remember.</li>
<li><strong>At checkout:</strong> a small QR card next to the payment terminal for clients who prefer to do it on the spot.</li>
</ul>
<p>Consistency is the point. If every client is asked, the process stays fair, and the reviews you receive reflect your real average experience, including the occasional three-star one, which actually makes the rest of the profile look more believable.</p>
<h2 id="what-to-say">What to Say (WhatsApp Scripts)</h2>
<p>Keep it short, personal and free of pressure. Adapt these to your salon&#8217;s voice:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Standard ask:</strong> &#8220;Hi [Name], thank you for visiting us today. If you enjoyed your appointment with [Stylist], we&#8217;d really appreciate a short Google review. Here&#8217;s the link: [link]. Thank you!&#8221;</li>
<li><strong>Specific nudge:</strong> &#8220;If you can mention the service you had, it helps other clients choose the right treatment.&#8221;</li>
<li><strong>Gentle reminder (once only):</strong> &#8220;Hi [Name], just a quick reminder in case you had a moment to share feedback. No worries if not.&#8221;</li>
</ul>
<p>Notice what the scripts don&#8217;t say: nothing about stars, nothing about a reward, and no pressure for a specific rating. You are asking for her honest experience, not a five.</p>
<p>If your salon already uses an automated WhatsApp flow, it&#8217;s a natural place to send the link after a completed appointment. Our <a href="/ai-appointment-booking-bot/">AI appointment booking bot</a> can send a post-visit message like this automatically, and our <a href="/reviews-reputation/">reviews and reputation service</a> covers the wider process of collecting reviews and responding to them.</p>
<div class="gd-cta-box gd-cta-mid">
<h3>Not sure how healthy your Google profile looks?</h3>
<p>We&#8217;ll review your reviews, replies and profile completeness for free and tell you the three changes worth making first.</p>
<p><a class="gd-cta-btn" href="/get-a-free-audit/">Get My Free Audit →</a>
</div>
<div class="gd-table-wrap">
<h4>Safe vs. risky ways to get salon reviews</h4>
<table class="gd-table">
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Approach</th>
<th>Safe?</th>
<th>Why</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Stylist asks at the chair and sends the link</td>
<td>Yes</td>
<td>Genuine request from the person who did the work</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>QR code card at reception or mirror</td>
<td>Yes</td>
<td>Matches Google&#8217;s own suggestion</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Discount or free add-on for a review</td>
<td>No</td>
<td>Incentivised reviews are prohibited as fake engagement</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Only messaging clients you expect to be happy</td>
<td>No</td>
<td>Review gating distorts genuine feedback</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Buying a bundle of reviews</td>
<td>No</td>
<td>Fake reviews can be removed and can put the profile at risk</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<h2 id="replying-to-reviews">Replying to Every Review</h2>
<p>Replying is part of the review system, not an extra. A prompt, specific reply shows future clients that someone is paying attention, and it gives you a chance to naturally mention the service in your own words.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Positive reviews:</strong> thank the client by first name, mention the service or stylist, and invite her back. Avoid copying the same sentence into every reply.</li>
<li><strong>Negative reviews:</strong> respond calmly, acknowledge the problem, and move the detail offline. Our <a href="/salon-bad-review-response-templates/">1-star review playbook</a> has word-for-word templates.</li>
<li><strong>Timing:</strong> aim for a reply within a day or two so the profile looks actively managed.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="mistakes-to-avoid">Mistakes That Get Reviews Removed</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Review spikes:</strong> twenty reviews on one day after months of silence looks unnatural. A steady trickle looks real because it is real.</li>
<li><strong>Staff reviewing their own salon:</strong> it&#8217;s a conflict of interest and can be flagged.</li>
<li><strong>Identical wording:</strong> copy-pasted reviews are a common sign of manipulation.</li>
<li><strong>Offering to &#8220;fix&#8221; a bad review for a reward:</strong> Google lists this alongside other incentives as prohibited.</li>
</ul>
<p>The bigger picture is that reviews are one lever in a larger system. They work best alongside a complete profile, good photos and a booking flow that doesn&#8217;t lose people afterwards; the full picture is in our <a href="/salon-marketing-dubai-guide/">complete salon marketing guide for Dubai</a>, and if enquiries arrive after hours, the <a href="/salon-whatsapp-booking-leak/">WhatsApp leak</a> is often the next thing to fix.</p>
<div class="gd-faq" id="salon-google-reviews-faq">
<h3>Frequently asked questions</h3>
<h4>Is it allowed to ask customers for Google reviews?</h4>
<p>Yes. Google&#8217;s own guidance tells businesses they can remind customers to leave reviews by sharing a Google review link or QR code. What is not allowed is offering anything in exchange for a review.</p>
<h4>Can I give a discount or free service for a review?</h4>
<p>No. Google treats incentives such as free or discounted goods or services in exchange for reviews as fake and misleading content, and strictly prohibits them. That includes offering a reward for changing or removing a negative review.</p>
<h4>Should I only ask happy clients for reviews?</h4>
<p>No. Filtering who you ask, so that only satisfied clients are sent to Google, is known as review gating and works against Google&#8217;s requirement that reviews reflect genuine experiences. Ask every client the same way and handle complaints separately and in person.</p>
<h4>When is the best moment to ask a salon client for a review?</h4>
<p>Right after the service, while the result is fresh, or in a short WhatsApp message later the same day. A personal, low-pressure request from the stylist converts better than a generic mass message.</p>
<h4>How many reviews does a salon need to rank in Google Maps?</h4>
<p>There is no fixed number. Google weighs relevance, distance and prominence, and reviews are one part of prominence. Steady, recent, genuine reviews with real detail matter more than a sudden spike.</p>
</div>
